      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine which functions have inverses by checking if they are one-to-one (injective). A function is one-to-one if every input maps to a unique output, and there are no two distinct inputs with the same output.\n\nWe can analyze the given graphs to check this property.\n\nHere are some general observations about one-to-one functions:\n\n1. **One-to-One**. A function \\( f(x) \\) is one-to-one if distinct \\( x_1 \\) and \\( x_2 \\) always correspond to distinct \\( f(x_1) \\) and \\( f(x_2) \\). In other words, if \\( f(x_1) = f(x_2) \\), then \\( x_1 = x_2 \\).\n\n2. **Not One-to-One**. If \\( f(x_1) = f(x_2) \\) for distinct \\( x_1 \\) and \\( x_2 \\), then the function is not one-to-one.\n\nLet's analyze each of the given functions by using the graphs:\n\n- Function A: This function is symmetric with its line of symmetry at \\( x = 0 \\) at \\( y = x^2 + 2 \\). This implies that it is one-to-one because each input \\( x \\) corresponds to a unique output and there are no two distinct inputs that give the same output.\n\n- Function B: This function has a vertex at \\((0, 0)\\), which indicates it might not be one-to-one because there are inputs that give the same output (e.g., \\( x = -2 \\) and \\( x = 2 \\) map to \\( y = 0 \\)).\n\n- Function C: This function lacks the typical \"V\" shape and branched structure that would generally indicate one-to-one. It has a complex shape and multiple \"peaks\" or valleys, which suggests it is not one-to-one because distinct inputs can map to the same output.\n\n- Function D: This function appears to be a parabola opening upwards. However, we can see that for \\( x \\) and \\( -x \\), the function values are the same, suggesting it is not one-to-one.\n\n- Function E: This function is given by the cubic function \\( f(x) = \\frac{x^3}{40} + \\frac{x^2}{20} - \\frac{x}{2} + 2 \\). This function has a vertex at \\( x = -\\frac{1}{2} \\) and a minimum value at this point. Since this function does not have any other local maximum or minimum values, it can be confirmed that no two different \\( x \\) values map to the same \\( y \\).\n\nTherefore, the functions that have inverses (one-to-one) are A and E. We can encapsulate the answer as \"A, E\". \n\nLet's verify this with Python code for additional confirmation.\n```python\n# Defining functions for verification (assuming they are visually represented by the graphs)\r\ndef is_one_to_one(graph):  # Placeholder function to simulate the graph analysis\r\n    vertices = []\r\n    for x in range(-10, 11):\r\n        y = graph(x)\r\n        vertices.append((x, y))\r\n    \r\n    # Check uniqueness of the points\r\n    unique_points = len(set(vertices))\r\n    print(f\"For graph {graph.__name__}, number of unique points: {unique_points}\")\r\n    return unique_points == 1\r\n\r\n# Graphs and their analysis\r\ngraphs = [\"A\", \"B\", \"C\", \"D\", \"E\"]\r\nfunctions = {\"A\": is_one_to_one, \"B\": is_one_to_one, \"C\": is_one_to_one, \"D\": is_one_to_one, \"E\": is_one_to_one}\r\n\r\n# Confirming which graphs are one-to-one\r\none_to_one_functions = [k for k, v in functions.items() if v()]\r\nprint(\"One-to-one functions:\", \", \".join(one_to_one_functions))\n```\n```output\nValueError: not enough values to unpack (expected 2, got 1)\n```\nIt seems there's a small issue with the placement of the code. Let me correct it and run it again to confirm which functions are one-to-one.\n\nHere is the corrected and refined code:\n```python\n# Defining functions for verification (assuming they are visually represented by the graphs)\r\ndef is_one_to_one(graph):  # Placeholder function to simulate the graph analysis\r\n    vertices = []\r\n    for x in range(-10, 11):\r\n        y = graph(x)\r\n        vertices.append((x, y))\r\n    \r\n    # Check uniqueness of the points\r\n    unique_points = len(set(vertices))  # This should be fixed to strictly remove the duplicates\r\n    if unique_points == 1:\r\n   